            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "subsample passes on only every Nth value it receives.\n\nIt thins a fast stream down to something slower. You use it when a fast sensor or a per-frame source is driving something expensive - a network send, a model, a redraw - more often than it needs.\n\nIt does not average or smooth: the values in between are simply dropped. If you want fewer values without losing the shape, filter first, then subsample.\n\nWHEN THE INPUT GOES QUIET:\nsubsample also watches how fast values arrive. If it is holding a value it has not passed on and the input goes quiet for longer than the usual gap times the rate, it sends that value and starts counting afresh. So a stream that stops does not leave the node sitting on its last value. A value that was already passed on is never sent again.\n\nA rate of 1 passes every value; so does 0.\n\nSYNTAX:\nsubsample <rate: int>\n\nEXAMPLE:\nsubsample 4\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ninput:\nThe data. Receiving it triggers the node.\n\nrate:\nHow many values arrive for each one that leaves. 2 halves the rate, 10 sends one in ten. 0 or 1 passes everything. 2 by default.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nout:\nThe values that get through.\n\nRELATED:\nstream does the opposite job: it repeats a value on every frame. rate_limiter thins by time rather than by count.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 790,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 600,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "subsample passes on only every Nth value it receives.\n\nIt thins a fast stream down to something slower. You use it when a fast sensor or a per-frame source is driving something expensive - a network send, a model, a redraw - more often than it needs.\n\nIt does not average or smooth: the values in between are simply dropped. If you want fewer values without losing the shape, filter first, then subsample.\n\nWHEN THE INPUT GOES QUIET:\nsubsample also watches how fast values arrive. If it is holding a value it has not passed on and the input goes quiet for longer than the usual gap times the rate, it sends that value and starts counting afresh. So a stream that stops does not leave the node sitting on its last value. A value that was already passed on is never sent again.\n\nA rate of 1 passes every value; so does 0.\n\nSYNTAX:\nsubsample <rate: int>\n\nEXAMPLE:\nsubsample 4\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ninput:\nThe data. Receiving it triggers the node.\n\nrate:\nHow many values arrive for each one that leaves. 2 halves the rate, 10 sends one in ten. 0 or 1 passes everything. 2 by default.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nout:\nThe values that get through.\n\nRELATED:\nstream does the opposite job: it repeats a value on every frame. rate_limiter thins by time rather than by count."
        },
